<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Improving Video Diffusion Transformer Training by Multi-Feature Fusion and Alignment from Self-Supervised Vision Encoders</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
Dohun Lee<span class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\equalcontrib</span><sup class="ltx_sup">1</sup>,
Hyeonho Jeong<span class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\equalcontrib</span><sup class="ltx_sup">1, 2</sup>,
Jiwook Kim<sup class="ltx_sup">1</sup>,
Duygu Ceylan<sup class="ltx_sup">2</sup>,
Jong Chul Ye<sup class="ltx_sup">1</sup>
</span></span>
</div>
<div class="ltx_abstract">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
<p class="ltx_p">Video diffusion models have advanced rapidly in the recent years as a result of series of architectural innovations (e.g., diffusion transformers) and use of novel training objectives (e.g., flow matching). In contrast, less attention has been paid to improving the feature representation power of such models. In this work, we show that training video diffusion models can benefit from aligning the intermediate features of the video generator with feature representations of pre-trained vision encoders. We propose a new metric and conduct an in-depth analysis of various vision encoders to evaluate their discriminability and temporal consistency, thereby assessing their suitability for video feature alignment.
Based on the analysis, we present Align4Gen which provides a novel multi-feature fusion and alignment method integrated into video diffusion model training. We evaluate Align4Gen both for unconditional and class-conditional video generation tasks and show that it results in improved video generation as quantified by various metrics. Full video results are available on our project page: <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">https://align4gen.github.io/align4gen/</span></p>
</div>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S1">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Video generative models have made rapid strides in recent years.
State-of-the-art video diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Polyak et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib31" title="">2024</a>; Brooks et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib2" title="">2024</a>; Kong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib21" title="">2024</a>; Yang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib54" title="">2024</a>; Ma et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib25" title="">2025</a>)</cite> have advanced in synthesizing high-resolution photorealistic videos that last several minutes.
To achieve these advancements, recent studies have focused on architectural innovations, e.g., transitioning from U-Net to Diffusion Transformers (DiT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Peebles and Xie <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib29" title="">2023</a>)</cite>, and rethinking the training objective, such as flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Lipman et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib22" title="">2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">In contrast, less effort has been made to analyze the correlation between the feature representation power of large video models and their generation capabilities. In fact, various recent works have demonstrated that large-scale diffusion models learn powerful and discriminative features that can facilitate various analysis tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Tang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib44" title="">2023</a>; Luo et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib24" title="">2023</a>; Zhang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib58" title="">2023</a>)</cite>.
Such works make two important observations: first, the features of generative models are still inferior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(El Banani et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib8" title="">2024</a>; Jeong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib18" title="">2024</a>)</cite> compared to the features of self-supervised vision models, such as DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Oquab et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib28" title="">2023</a>)</cite>. More interestingly, the features of the generative models and self-supervised vision models tend to exhibit complementary properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Zhang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib58" title="">2023</a>)</cite>. Inspired by these observations,
REPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Yu et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib55" title="">2024</a>)</cite> demonstrated that aligning the internal representations of DiT with an external pre-trained visual encoder during training significantly improves both discriminative and generative performance.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">Given the success of REPA,
we hypothesize that pre-trained self-supervised vision models can be used as guidance when training video diffusion models to ensure they learn more discriminative features.
Moreover, we assume that operating at a more discriminative feature space would facilitate higher quality video generation.
Consequently, in this work, we introduce Align4Gen, a novel framework that exploits the rich representation of pre-trained vision encoders to improve video diffusion training.
We begin by proposing a novel metric that quantifies both discriminability and temporal invariance, and use it to analyze features from a variety of pre-trained image and video encoders.
Surprisingly, image-trained encoders often exhibit superior discriminability and temporal consistency.
We further observe that different pre-trained image features, such as DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Oquab et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib28" title="">2023</a>)</cite> and SAM2.1 Hiera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ravi et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib33" title="">2024</a>)</cite>, focus on capturing different frequency signals and hence <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exhibit complementary characteristics</span>.
we design a multi-feature fusion and alignment strategy that aligns intermediate features of video diffusion transformers with those from multiple pre-trained image encoders.
We validate Align4Gen on both unconditional and class-conditional video generation tasks, demonstrating consistent improvements across a range of evaluation metrics and datasets.
Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul class="ltx_itemize" id="S1.I1">
<li class="ltx_item" id="S1.I1.i1" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.I1.i1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We propose a novel metric for assessing both discriminability and temporal consistency of pre-trained vision-encoder features, and use it to systematically analyze their suitability as guidance for video diffusion.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S1.I1.i2" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.I1.i2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We present Align4Gen, a multi-feature fusion and alignment strategy that integrates multiple, but complementary image encoder representations (across frequencies) into video diffusion training.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="ltx_item" id="S1.I1.i3" style="list-style-type:none;">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S1.I1.i3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We validate Align4Gen with extensive experiments, demonstrating consistent gains in generation quality and accelerated training on both unconditional and class-conditional video diffusion tasks.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S2">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Video Diffusion Models.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Inspired by the success of diffusion models in image generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Rombach et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib34" title="">2022</a>; Podell et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib30" title="">2023</a>)</cite>, early approaches in video generation adapted existing image diffusion architectures by incorporating a temporal dimension, enabling models to be trained on both image and video data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Blattmann et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib1" title="">2023</a>; Singer et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib38" title="">2022</a>; Guo et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib12" title="">2023</a>)</cite>.
Typically, U-Net-based architectures add temporal attention blocks after spatial attention blocks and expand 2D convolutions to 3D to capture temporal dynamics.
More recently, the introduction of DiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Peebles and Xie <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib29" title="">2023</a>)</cite>, combined with large-scale, high-quality curated datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ju et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib19" title="">2025</a>; Wang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib50" title="">2023</a>; Nan et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib27" title="">2024</a>)</cite>, has led to more efficient and scalable diffusion generators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ma et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib26" title="">2024</a>; Yang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib54" title="">2024</a>; Zhou et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib60" title="">2024</a>; Brooks et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib2" title="">2024</a>)</cite>, significantly advancing video generation quality.
Furthermore, rectified flows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Lipman et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib22" title="">2022</a>; Esser et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib9" title="">2024</a>; Chen et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib6" title="">2018</a>)</cite> have shown faster training convergence and inference speed, making them increasingly popular for both image and video generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Esser et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib9" title="">2024</a>; Zheng et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib59" title="">2024</a>; Kong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib21" title="">2024</a>; HaCohen et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib13" title="">2024</a>)</cite>.
Following this wave, we build our method on a video diffusion transformer (V-DiT) architecture and demonstrate its significant efficiency under both diffusion-based and flow-based training paradigms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion Training with Auxiliary Loss.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">To enhance diffusion model training, various strategies incorporating auxiliary losses have been proposed. Notably, recent studies have shown that diffusion models inherently learn highly discriminative representations within their features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Tang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib44" title="">2023</a>; Luo et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib24" title="">2023</a>; Zhang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib58" title="">2023</a>)</cite>. Building on this insight, REPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Yu et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib55" title="">2024</a>)</cite> aligns diffusion features with external pretrained visual encoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Oquab et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib28" title="">2023</a>; He et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib14" title="">2021</a>)</cite> to improve image diffusion training.
More recently, a range of efforts have been made to enhance video diffusion training.
Track4Gen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Jeong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib18" title="">2024</a>)</cite> identifies a strong correlation between pixel-space appearance inconsistencies and feature-space temporal inconsistencies—i.e., video point tracking failures—and demonstrates that adding dense point tracking loss to video diffusion features significantly improves generation quality.
Similarly, JOG3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Huang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib17" title="">2025</a>)</cite> integrates a camera pose estimation loss into video diffusion training, showing that video generation and pose estimation can be jointly optimized.
Additionally, GenRec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Weng et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib52" title="">2025</a>)</cite> performs joint training of video generation and video recognition, leveraging mutual reinforcement between these tasks.
VideoJAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Chefer et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib4" title="">2025</a>)</cite> enhances motion coherence by incorporating an optical flow denoising loss, explicitly encoding a strong motion prior into a video diffusion transformer.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">Although these works demonstrate that leveraging the auxiliary loss can support the training of video diffusion models, the potential of feature learning with powerful pretrained vision encoders remains underexplored.
In this paper, we investigate whether our proposed additional loss, guided by pretrained vision encoders, eases learning features for video diffusion models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Self-supervised Vision Encoders.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Self-supervised learning (SSL) methods have demonstrated remarkable success in vision tasks by learning effective feature representations without human-annotated labels. These methods can be broadly categorized into contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Caron et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib3" title="">2021</a>; Oquab et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib28" title="">2023</a>)</cite>, masked image modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(He et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib14" title="">2021</a>; Tong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib45" title="">2022</a>)</cite>, and predictive modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib49" title="">2024</a>; Weinzaepfel et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib51" title="">2022</a>; Zhang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib57" title="">2024</a>)</cite>.
Many existing studies have not only focused on learning high-quality representations but have also delved into analyzing the features extracted from different vision encoders to gain deeper insights into their underlying structure. Taking a similar approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Stevens et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib43" title="">2025</a>)</cite>, some methods employ a sparse autoencoder to extract interpretable features from vision models. Inspired by this, we perform feature frequency analysis to classify feature characteristics and develop a training framework that enhances generative video diffusion transformer features by leveraging these traits.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S3">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Align4Gen</h2>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S3.F1"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="379" id="S3.F1.g1" src="figs/IICR.png" width="598"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IICR comparisons.</span>
We present IICR values for different pre-trained vision encoders across varying number of clusters(k) in K-Means over 1,000 videos. DUSt3R and VideoMAE show a decline in IICR as k increases, indicating weaker discriminative power relative to temporal consistency. In contrast, DINOv2 and SAM2 maintain higher values, with DINOv2 slightly decreasing due to its focus on semantics over high-frequency details.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S3.SSx1">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Preliminaries</h3>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diffusion Models.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Sohl-Dickstein et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib40" title="">2015</a>; Ho, Jain, and Abbeel <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib16" title="">2020</a>)</cite> generate data by gradually corrupting a clean sample with Gaussian noise and then learning to reverse this noising process. In the forward process, a data sample <math alttext="{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is progressively corrupted over <math alttext="T" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps. At each time step <math alttext="t" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, a Gaussian transition is defined as follows:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E1">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="q({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}\!\left({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1},\,\beta_{t}\mathbf{I}\right)" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E1.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}\!\left({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1},\,\beta_{t}\mathbf{I}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="\{\beta_{t}\}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed noise scheduler. Using the properties of Gaussian distributions, the marginal distribution <math alttext="q({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}\mid{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in a closed form.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">In the reverse diffusion process, the model aims to recover <math alttext="{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by denoising <math alttext="{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> step by step. Instead of directly predicting <math alttext="{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the network <math alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\theta}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t},t)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\theta}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to estimate the noise <math alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> that was injected during the forward process. The training objective can be expressed as follows:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E2">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="L_{\mathrm{diff}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0},\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\!\left[\left\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\theta}\!\Bigl{(}\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon},\,t\Bigr{)}\right\|^{2}_{2}\right]" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E2.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>diff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{diff}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0},\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\!\left[\left\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}-\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\theta}\!\Bigl{(}\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}+\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon},\,t\Bigr{)}\right\|^{2}_{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="\bar{\alpha}_{t}=\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_{s})" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}_{t}=\prod_{s=1}^{t}(1-\beta_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>. This formulation of <math alttext="\epsilon" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> prediction loss has been shown to stabilize training and produce high quality samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Flow Models.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Recently, flow-based training frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Liu, Gong, and Liu <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib23" title="">2022</a>; Esser et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib9" title="">2024</a>; Chen et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib7" title="">2025</a>; Zheng et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib59" title="">2024</a>)</cite> have gained attention for their ability to progressively transform samples from a prior distribution to the target data distribution through a series of linear interpolations.
Specifically, we define a velocity field <math alttext="v_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}})" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> of a flow <math alttext="\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}):[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}):[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math alttext="\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})={\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})={\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math alttext="\psi_{1}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})=x_{1}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})=x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, the <math alttext="\psi_{t}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely characterized by a flow ODE:</p>
<table class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table" id="A7.EGx1">
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math alttext="\displaystyle d\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}})=v_{t}(\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}))dt" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.E3.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}})=v_{t}(\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}))dt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">In particular, linear conditional flow defines the flow as <math alttext="{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}=\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{1}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})=(1-t){\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}+t{\boldsymbol{x}}_{1}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}=\psi_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{1}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})=(1-t){\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}+t{\boldsymbol{x}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we can compute the velocity field <math alttext="v_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})=\dot{\psi}_{t}(\psi_{t}^{-1}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})={\boldsymbol{x}}_{1}-{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ψ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})=\dot{\psi}_{t}(\psi_{t}^{-1}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t}|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})|{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0})={\boldsymbol{x}}_{1}-{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to the
following conditional flow matching loss:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E4">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="L_{\mathrm{RF}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0},\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\left[\left\|({\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}-\boldsymbol{\epsilon})-v_{\theta}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t},t)\right\|^{2}\right].\vskip-2.84526pt" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E4.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>RF</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{RF}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,\,{\boldsymbol{x}}_{0},\,\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})}\left[\left\|({\boldsymbol{x}}_{0}-\boldsymbol{\epsilon})-v_{\theta}({\boldsymbol{x}}_{t},t)\right\|^{2}\right].\vskip-2.84526pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">During inference, the predicted velocity is utilized to guide the transformation of an initial noise sample towards the target data distribution through a reverse integration process. This approach enables efficient and controlled generation of high-quality samples.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S3.F2"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="426" id="S3.F2.g1" src="figs/pca_fig.png" width="598"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PCA visualization of different vision encoder features.</span>
Pretrained image encoders—DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Oquab et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib28" title="">2023</a>)</cite> and SAM2 Hiera image encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ravi et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib33" title="">2024</a>)</cite>—yield feature representations that remain consistent across frames for both foreground objects and background.
In contrast, video or 3D vision encoders such as VideoMAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Tong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib45" title="">2022</a>)</cite> and DUSt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib49" title="">2024</a>)</cite> exhibit significant temporal inconsistencies in their feature representations.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S3.SSx2">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Analysis of Features</h3>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">In order to find optimal vision encoders that accelerate video diffusion model training,
we begin by analyzing the features of the pretrained image, video, and 3D encoders as well as the features of DiTs.</p>
</div>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Discriminative Power and Temporal Consistency.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">An effective pretrained vision encoder for video models should possess two key properties: (1) strong discriminative power and (2) temporal consistency. This is especially crucial in our setting, where the video diffusion model employs a 2D VAE to produce frame-wise independent latents and unpatchifies each frame independently at the final layer of the DiT. In this setup, it is essential that temporally aligned objects yield similar features regardless of their frame positions. To jointly quantify these two properties, we propose a novel metric, <span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Intra-Inter Consistency Ratio (IICR)</span>, to evaluate the suitability for video diffusion model training.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">Given a video, we extract per-frame feature representations using a pretrained vision encoder.
Let <math alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the centroid (mean embedding) of cluster <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
We define the inter-cluster distance as</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E5">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="D_{\text{inter}}=\min\{\|\mathbf{u}_{i}-\mathbf{u}_{j}\|\mid i\neq j\}.\vskip-2.84526pt" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E5.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>∣</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{inter}}=\min\{\|\mathbf{u}_{i}-\mathbf{u}_{j}\|\mid i\neq j\}.\vskip-2.84526pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">which captures the smallest separation between distinct clusters; larger <math alttext="D_{\text{inter}}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{inter}}</annotation></semantics></math> implies more discriminative features.
We then define the intra-cluster distance <math alttext="D_{\text{intra}}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{intra}}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E6">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="D_{\text{intra}}=\max\{\sigma_{i}\mid i\text{ in number of clusters}\},\vskip-2.84526pt" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E6.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in number of clusters</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{intra}}=\max\{\sigma_{i}\mid i\text{ in number of clusters}\},\vskip-2.84526pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="\sigma_{i}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard deviation of embeddings within cluster <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Larger <math alttext="D_{\text{intra}}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{intra}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates poorer temporal consistency.
Finally, we define the Intra-Inter Consistency Ratio (IICR) as</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E7">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="\text{IICR}=\frac{D_{\text{inter}}}{D_{\text{intra}}}.\vskip-2.84526pt" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E7.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>IICR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>inter</mtext></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>intra</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{IICR}=\frac{D_{\text{inter}}}{D_{\text{intra}}}.\vskip-2.84526pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">A higher IICR indicates that the pre-trained encoder produces features that are simultaneously discriminative (large inter-cluster separation) and temporally stable (small intra-cluster variation).</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">Using the IICR metric, we evaluate four vision encoders: the image-based DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Oquab et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib28" title="">2023</a>)</cite> and SAM2.1 Hiera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ravi et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib33" title="">2024</a>)</cite>, the video encoder VideoMAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Tong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib45" title="">2022</a>)</cite>, and DUSt3R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Wang et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib49" title="">2024</a>)</cite>, which is trained for 3D reconstruction.
As illustrated in Fig. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Align4Gen ‣ Improving Video Diffusion Transformer Training by Multi-Feature Fusion and Alignment from Self-Supervised Vision Encoders"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, both DINOv2 and SAM2.1 Hiera consistently achieve high IICR scores across varying <math alttext="K" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, VideoMAE and DUSt3R degrade noticeably as <math alttext="K" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases, indicating a loss in fine-grained discriminability.
We corroborate this with PCA visualizations in Fig. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Flow Models. ‣ Preliminaries ‣ 3 Align4Gen ‣ Improving Video Diffusion Transformer Training by Multi-Feature Fusion and Alignment from Self-Supervised Vision Encoders"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>:
the image-level encoders (DINOv2 and SAM2.1 Hiera) produce stable, consistent color assignments for the same backgrounds and subjects across frames, reflecting strong temporal invariance, whereas VideoMAE and the 3D-aware DUSt3R exhibit substantial instability and fluctuations.
These results motivate our selection of DINOv2 and SAM2.1 Hiera as the primary sources for feature alignment in video diffusion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Frequency Distribution.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Given the candidate image-level encoders <math alttext="\mathcal{E}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, we analyze the frequency distribution of their extracted features
<math alttext="\mathbf{F}=\mathcal{E}(x_{0})\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times c}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}=\mathcal{E}(x_{0})\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times c}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first transform the features into the frequency domain via a 2D Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) and compute the log-magnitude spectrum:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E8">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="\mathbf{S}=\mathrm{FFT}_{\mathrm{2D}}(\mathbf{F})," class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E8.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>FFT</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}=\mathrm{FFT}_{\mathrm{2D}}(\mathbf{F}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E9">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="\mathbf{M}=\log\left(|\mathbf{S}|+\epsilon\right)," class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E9.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}=\log\left(|\mathbf{S}|+\epsilon\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="\epsilon" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant for numerical stability.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">We then measure the mean log-magnitude difference between the lowest- and highest-frequency regions.
The lowest-frequency (DC) component is given by:</p>
<table class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table" id="A7.EGx2">
<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Lowest frequency:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{M}_{0,0}," class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.E10.m2"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{M}_{0,0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="(0,0)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,0)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the center of the Fourier spectrum.
The high-frequency (HF) region is defined as:</p>
<table class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table" id="A7.EGx3">
<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">High-frequency region:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}=\left\{(u,v)\,\middle|\,\sqrt{u^{2}+v^{2}}&gt;0.75\,r\right\}," class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.E11.m2"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}=\left\{(u,v)\,\middle|\,\sqrt{u^{2}+v^{2}}&gt;0.75\,r\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="r=h/2" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=h/2</annotation></semantics></math> for a square input of size <math alttext="h\times h" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\times h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">The frequency difference metric is computed as</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E12">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{freq}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}|}\sum_{(u,v)\in\mathcal{H}}\mathbf{M}_{u,v}-\mathbf{M}_{0,0}." class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E12.m1"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>freq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{freq}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{H}|}\sum_{(u,v)\in\mathcal{H}}\mathbf{M}_{u,v}-\mathbf{M}_{0,0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p4">
<p class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Frequency Distribution. ‣ Analysis of Features ‣ 3 Align4Gen ‣ Improving Video Diffusion Transformer Training by Multi-Feature Fusion and Alignment from Self-Supervised Vision Encoders"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the value <math alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{freq}}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>freq</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{freq}}</annotation></semantics></math> for DINOv2 and SAM2.1 Hiera differs by <math alttext="0.3" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math> in log scale, corresponding to a <math alttext="{\times}1.35" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1.35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\times}1.35</annotation></semantics></math> ratio.
This indicates a clear gap in their frequency composition: DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Oquab et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib28" title="">2023</a>)</cite> predominantly captures low-frequency, semantic structures, while the Hiera <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ryali et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib37" title="">2023</a>)</cite> encoder in SAM2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ravi et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib33" title="">2024</a>)</cite> exhibits stronger sensitivity to high-frequency details.
Given the complementary nature of these features, we incorporate both into our video diffusion training, as discussed in the following section.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S3.F3"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="282" id="S3.F3.g1" src="figs/frequency_final.png" width="598"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mean log differences between the lowest and highest frequency components.</span> DINOv2 focuses more on low-frequency components, whereas SAM2.1 Hiera places greater emphasis on high-frequency regions. We also plot the same metric for the features obtained from a mid-block of a video diffusion transformer model as a reference.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S3.SSx3">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Video DiT Training with Feature Alignment</h3>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Guided by the prior analysis, we design a training framework that enforces consistency between the intermediate tokens of a video diffusion transformer (V-DiT) and representations from pre-trained image encoders.</p>
</div>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Patch Token Alignment.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">At the heart of our method is <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">patch token alignment</em>, which regularizes V-DiT by aligning its patch-level tokens with those extracted from a pre-trained image transformer <math alttext="T_{I}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each video frame, <math alttext="T_{I}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> produces local patch embeddings <math alttext="p_{i}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and V-DiT produces corresponding tokens <math alttext="q_{i}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math alttext="i" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the patch or token index.
To bridge the gap between their feature spaces, we pass <math alttext="q_{i}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> through a lightweight mapper—implemented as a multi-layer perceptron (MLP)—before alignment.
Feature alignment is then enforced by minimizing:
</p>
<table class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table" id="A7.EGx4">
<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell(p_{i},\text{MLP}(q_{i}))" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.E13.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>MLP</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\ell(p_{i},\text{MLP}(q_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="\ell(\cdot,\cdot)" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a distance measure (e.g., cosine distance) and <math alttext="N" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of patch tokens.
The full training objective augments the base video denoising loss (diffusion or flow-based) with the alignment term:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E14">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{diff/RF}}+\gamma\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E14.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>diff/RF</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{diff/RF}}+\gamma\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">where <math alttext="\gamma" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> balances the contribution of the alignment regularizer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Feature Fusion.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We further enhance training by aligning V-DiT features with a fused representation constructed from multiple complementary image encoders.
Different encoders capture distinct frequency characteristics—e.g., DINOv2 emphasizes low-frequency semantics, while SAM2.1 Hiera captures high-frequency detail—so we combine their strengths.
Given a set of encoder outputs <math alttext="F_{1},F_{2},\dots,F_{n}" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S3.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1},F_{2},\dots,F_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a unified feature representation via concatenation along the feature dimension:</p>
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table" id="S3.E15">
<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math alttext="F=[F_{1};F_{2};\dots;F_{n}]" class="ltx_Math" display="block" id="S3.E15.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=[F_{1};F_{2};\dots;F_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right" rowspan="1"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td>
</tr></tbody>
</table>
<p class="ltx_p">producing a richer, multi-frequency supervisory signal.
This aggregated representation enforces consistency across diverse feature sources and preserves both coarse and fine-grained information.
In specific, we integrate DINOv2 and SAM2.1 Hiera, leveraging their complementary strengths—DINOv2 captures low-frequency semantics, while SAM2.1 Hiera focuses on high-frequency details.
This combination enables a more holistic representation enables capturing nuanced details beyond mere high-level semantics.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section class="ltx_section" id="S4">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">
<span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S4.SSx1">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Experiment Setup</h3>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation details.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We validate the performance of Align4Gen in both unconditional and class-conditional video generation tasks.
For class-conditional video generation, we implement the V-DiT model at two scales, V-DiT-L and V-DiT-XL, following previous practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ma et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib26" title="">2024</a>)</cite>. The patch size of 2 is utilized for all V-DiT models.
V-DiT-L consists of <math alttext="24" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math> transformer blocks with a total of 466M parameters in its transformer backbone, while V-DiT-XL comprises <math alttext="28" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2"><semantics><mn>28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28</annotation></semantics></math> transformer blocks with 683M parameters.
We use the UCF-101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Soomro, Zamir, and Shah <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib42" title="">2012</a>)</cite>, SkyTimelapse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Xiong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib53" title="">2018</a>)</cite>, and FaceForensics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Rössler et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib35" title="">2018</a>)</cite> datasets for training, with videos at a uniform resolution of <math alttext="256\times 256\times 16" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256\times 16</annotation></semantics></math>.
We train V-DiT-L for 1M steps with a batch size of 24 and V-DiT-XL for 200K steps with a batch size of 16.
For video sampling after training, we use the DDIM sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Song, Meng, and Ermon <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib41" title="">2020</a>)</cite> with 50 steps for diffusion-based models and the Euler sampler with 50 steps for flow-based models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We compare our method (denoted as <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">‘V-DiT + Ours’</em>) to several baselines. First, we train the V-DiT-L and V-DiT-XL models using only the standard diffusion or flow loss which we denote as <em class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">‘V-DiT’</em>.
Next, we also train the V-DiT models using only a single pretrained image encoder for feature alignment, either DINOv2 or SAM2.1 Hiera.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation metrics.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">We assess video generation quality using Fréchet Video Distance (FVD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Unterthiner et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib47" title="">2019</a>)</cite>, Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Heusel et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib15" title="">2018</a>)</cite>, Inception Score (IS) , and frame-wise CLIP similarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Radford et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib32" title="">2021</a>)</cite>.
Classical FVD computed with the I3D backbone tends to emphasize per-frame fidelity over motion.
To counterbalance this bias and better capture both content and temporal dynamics, we adopt the content-debiased FVD variant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Ge et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib10" title="">2024</a>)</cite> using VideoMAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Tong et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib45" title="">2022</a>)</cite> as the feature extractor.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_figure" id="S4.F4"><img alt="Refer to caption" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" height="685" id="S4.F4.g1" src="x1.png" width="969"/>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative comparisons on UCF-101</span>. Our method converges noticeably faster than the original model. Moreover, in addition to improved per-frame image quality, the X-T slice analysis reveals smoother frame transitions, highlighting enhanced visual fidelity and motion consistency in the V-DiT model. Here, the green line indicates the position of the X-T slice, and videos in the same row share the same green line.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section class="ltx_subsection" id="S4.SSx2">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Results</h3>
<section class="ltx_paragraph" id="S4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Class-conditional Video Generation.</h4>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1">
<p class="ltx_p">Tab. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Class-conditional Video Generation. ‣ Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Video Diffusion Transformer Training by Multi-Feature Fusion and Alignment from Self-Supervised Vision Encoders"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrates that Align4Gen yields substantial gains in both FVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Unterthiner et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib47" title="">2019</a>)</cite> and FID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(Heusel et al. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#bib.bib15" title="">2018</a>)</cite> over the vanilla V-DiT baseline, reflecting improvements in overall video fidelity as well as per-frame image quality.
Moreover, aligning with a single image encoder (V-DiT + Ours (DINOv2) or V-DiT + Ours (SAM2)) already outperforms the original model, while fusing DINOv2 and SAM2.1 Hiera (V-DiT + Ours (Fusion) delivers the best FVD and FID.
Notably, the fusion variant at 400K steps surpasses the baseline at 1M steps, indicating at least a <math alttext="\times 2.5" class="ltx_Math" display="inline" id="S4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1"><semantics><mrow><mi></mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 2.5</annotation></semantics></math> faster convergence, substantially reducing the computational cost of video diffusion model training.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p2">
<p class="ltx_p">Feature alignment does not compromise diversity: the Inception Score increases, implying quality gains are not due to mode collapse.
For temporal consistency (measured via frame-wise CLIP similarity), Align4Gen consistently matches or exceeds the vanilla baseline, with differences among non-baseline variants being negligible—showing maintained scene-level coherence.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_para" id="S4.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p3">
<p class="ltx_p">Qualitatively (Fig. <a class="ltx_ref" href="https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09547v1#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Evaluation metrics. ‣ Experiment Setup ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Video Diffusion Transformer Training by Multi-Feature Fusion and Alignment from Self-Supervised Vision Encoders"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), V-DiT + Ours (Fusion) produces sharper and more anatomically coherent human structures, and X–T slice visualizations reveal smoother motion compared to the baseline.</p>
</div>
<figure class="ltx_table" id="S4.T1">
<p class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">
